A recirculation apparatus comprising:

a body defining a first passage configured to receive exhaust gas, a second passage configured to convey the exhaust gas to an intake flow path, and a third passage configured to receive pressurized fuel;

a piston disposed within the body and movable along a longitudinal axis between a first position and a second position, the piston having an exhaust-pressure-responsive surface in fluid communication with the first passage such that exhaust pressure acting upon the surface longitudinally actuates the piston;

a valve portion operatively coupled to the piston and configured to selectively establish fluid communication between the first passage and the second passage in response to longitudinal displacement of the piston; and

a nozzle in fluid communication with the third passage, the nozzle defining a metering orifice sized to regulate a rate of fuel flow discharged from the nozzle,

wherein variation in the exhaust pressure causes corresponding movement of the piston to control recirculation of exhaust gas through the body, and wherein the metering orifice restricts the pressurized fuel to provide a predetermined fuel-flow characteristic.

1. A device for mixing exhaust gas with fuel, the device comprising:  
a housing defining an exhaust passage and a nozzle cavity in fluid communication with the exhaust passage, the nozzle cavity having a throat defining a smallest cross-sectional flow area of the nozzle cavity;  
a fuel passage configured to deliver fuel to the throat;  
a piston movably disposed in a piston chamber and having a first surface exposed to exhaust gas such that pressure of the exhaust gas displaces the piston; and  
a fuel-control member operatively coupled to the piston and movable in response to displacement of the piston to regulate delivery of the fuel through the throat, whereby the fuel is introduced into and mixed with the exhaust gas in the nozzle cavity.
